<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Timaeus of Tauromenium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 345–c. 250 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Timaeus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Tauromenium</a> was a Greek historian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, the son of the city's founder Andromachus. Exiled by the tyrant Agathocles, he spent about fifty years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where he wrote a long history of Sicily and the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> down to 264 BCE. His work, lost except for fragments, was much used by later writers and helped establish dating by Olympiads.</p></div>
